# ocspcheck — PKI / OCSP troubleshooting tool

A single-file script for production support. Give it a certificate (or a live
`host:port`) and it tells you, in one run, whether revocation checking for that
certificate works and where it is slow or broken: DNS, TCP, HTTP, the OCSP
responder, or the CRL fallback.

It is the scripted version of the manual checks used in the October 2026
`ngocsp.citi.com` timeout investigation.

## Contents

| File | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `ocspcheck.py` | The tool. Python 3.6+, standard library only, calls the `openssl` command. |
| `ocspcheck.sh` | Older bash version, same checks, text output only. Kept for boxes without Python 3. |
| `ocsp-servers.json` | Servers file for our environment (`pkivamwp01`, `pkivamwp02`, `ngocsp.citi.com`). |
| `README.md` | This file. |

## Requirements

- Python 3.6 or newer (`python3 --version`) — RHEL 8 and later ship this.
- `openssl` on the PATH (any 1.1.1 or 3.x).
- No root, no pip, no network access other than to the servers being tested.

```
chmod +x ocspcheck.py
./ocspcheck.py --help
```

## What it checks

1. **Certificate** — subject, issuer, serial, validity and days left, SANs, key
   usage, and the three URLs that matter: OCSP (AIA), CA issuer (AIA) and CRL
   distribution point.
2. **Issuer** — gets the issuing CA cert (from `-i`, the TLS chain, the servers
   file, or the AIA download) and confirms it actually signed the leaf. A wrong
   issuer is the usual reason for an `unauthorized` OCSP answer.
3. **OCSP** — for each responder, tests the layers separately so the report
   names the broken one:
   - DNS resolution (time, resolved addresses)
   - TCP connect (time, refused / timed out)
   - the OCSP exchange itself (time, `good` / `revoked` / `unknown` /
     `unauthorized` / HTTP error / timeout)
   - responder ID and next-update time on success
4. **CRL fallback** — downloads the CRL clients would fall back to: size, entry
   count, download time, stale or not, and whether this serial is on it.
5. **Summary** — verdict, counts, and an exit code for automation.

## Usage

```
ocspcheck.py <cert.pem | cert.cer | host:port> [options]

  -i, --issuer FILE      issuer CA certificate (skips the AIA download)
  -o, --ocsp URL         extra OCSP responder to test (repeatable)
  --servers FILE         JSON file listing our PKI servers (see below)
  --ignore-aia           ignore the OCSP / CA-issuer URLs inside the cert;
                         test only --servers and -o
  -t, --timeout SECS     per-request timeout (default 10)
  --no-crl               skip the CRL download
  --json                 JSON output
  --html                 HTML report
  -O, --output FILE      write the report to FILE instead of stdout
  --no-color             plain text (no ANSI colours)
```

Certificates may be PEM or DER (`.pem`, `.cer`, `.crt` — Windows "Copy to
File" output works as-is).

